# Methods

## Participants

::: {.content-visible when-meta="blinded"}
This study uses secondary data from the 2023 and 2024 waves of a panel survey on education and meritocracy [name withheld for anonymous review]. The panel examines school-age students’ beliefs, attitudes, and behaviors regarding meritocracy, inequality, and citizenship.
:::

::: {.content-hidden when-meta="blinded"}
This study uses secondary data from the 2023 and 2024 waves of the Panel Survey on Education and Meritocracy (EDUMER). The panel examines school-age students’ beliefs, attitudes, and behaviors regarding meritocracy, inequality, and citizenship.
:::

The questionnaire was administered to sixth-grade and ninth-grade students (primary and secondary cohorts, respectively) in nine schools in Chile’s Metropolitan and Valparaíso regions, a smaller number than the 15 schools originally planned, owing to refusals from selected schools. Although the sample was non-probabilistic and lacked quota controls, a minimum target of 900 students was set to ensure adequate statistical power. After data processing and listwise deletion, the analytical sample included 846 students in Wave 1 and 662 in Wave 2 (see @tbl-cohort for age and gender by cohort). Of the 846 Wave 1 students, 591 also had complete data in Wave 2 (linked retention = 69.9%), and the Wave 2 sample includes 71 students without complete Wave 1 data. The main sources of attrition were school transfers between academic years and high absenteeism in several schools. Within cohorts, the Wave 1 item means of retained and non-retained students differed by at most 0.14 points on the four-point scale.

```{r}
#| tbl-cap: Descriptive statistics for cohort level
#| tbl-cap-location: top
#| tbl-pos: H
#| echo: false
#| label: tbl-cohort
#| message: false
#| warning: false


tot <- db_long %>% 
  na.omit() %>% 
  group_by(ola, cohort_level) %>% 
  tally() %>% 
  rename(n_total = n) %>% 
  mutate(ola = if_else(ola == "1", "Wave 1", "Wave 2")) %>% 
  ungroup()

tot <- tot %>% 
    add_row(
      ola = "Wave 1",
      cohort_level = "Total",
      n_total = sum(tot[c(1,2),3])
    ) %>% 
  add_row(
      ola = "Wave 2",
      cohort_level = "Total",
      n_total = sum(tot[c(3,4),3]))


edad <- db_long %>% 
  na.omit() %>% 
  group_by(ola, cohort_level) %>% 
  summarise(m = round(mean(age),2),
            sd = round(sd(age),2),
            .groups = "drop_last") %>% 
  mutate(ola = if_else(ola == "1", "Wave 1", "Wave 2")) %>% 
  ungroup()

edad_ola <- db_long %>% 
  na.omit() %>% 
  group_by(ola) %>% 
  summarise(m = round(mean(age),2),
            sd = round(sd(age),2),
            .groups = "drop_last") %>% 
  mutate(ola = if_else(ola == "1", "Wave 1", "Wave 2"),
         cohort_level = "Total") %>% 
  select(ola, cohort_level, m, sd)


edad <- bind_rows(edad, edad_ola)

gen <- db_long %>% 
  na.omit() %>% 
  group_by(ola, cohort_level, p20) %>% 
  tally() %>% 
  ungroup() %>% 
  pivot_wider(id_cols = c(ola, cohort_level),
              names_from = p20,
              values_from = n) %>% 
  mutate(ola = if_else(ola == "1", "Wave 1", "Wave 2"))

gen_ola <- db_long %>% 
  na.omit() %>% 
  group_by(ola, p20) %>% 
  tally() %>% 
  ungroup() %>% 
  pivot_wider(id_cols = c(ola),
              names_from = p20,
              values_from = n) %>% 
  mutate(ola = if_else(ola == "1", "Wave 1", "Wave 2"),
         cohort_level = "Total") %>% 
  select(ola, cohort_level, H, M, O)

gen <- bind_rows(gen, gen_ola)

left_join(tot, edad) %>% 
  left_join(., gen) %>% 
  arrange(ola) %>% 
   knitr::kable(., format = table_format, escape = T,
                booktabs = T, 
                linesep = "",
                col.names = c("Wave", "level", "N", "Mean", "SD", "Man", "Women", "Others")) %>%
    kableExtra::add_header_above(c(" " = 3, "Age" = 2, "Gender" = 3)) %>%
   kableExtra::kable_styling(
     full_width = F,
     latex_options = c("HOLD_position", "scale_down"),
     position = "center",
     font_size = 9.5,
     bootstrap_options=c("striped", "bordered")) %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 1, width = "1.8cm") %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 2, width = "2.2cm") %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 3, width = "1.6cm") %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 4, width = "1.6cm") %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 5, width = "1.6cm") %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 6, width = "1.9cm") %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 7, width = "1.9cm") %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 8, width = "1.9cm") %>%
  kableExtra::collapse_rows(1)

```


## Procedure

Data were collected by a professional research firm using a Computer-Assisted Web Interviewing (CAWI) approach, based on online questionnaires. Written informed consent was obtained for all participants: students signed the consent form, which was reviewed and approved by a parent or legal guardian, and only students with signed consent were included in the study. To enable longitudinal linkage while protecting confidentiality, the survey firm generated anonymous unique identifiers for each student, and no directly identifying information was included in the analytic files. The analytic code and data are available as described in the data availability statement.

::: {.content-visible when-meta="blinded"}
The study was approved by the Research Ethics Committee of [institution withheld for anonymous review] (reference number withheld for anonymous review), which determined that the project posed minimal risk and approved the consent and confidentiality procedures. The hypotheses were preregistered on the Open Science Framework (OSF) prior to data analysis  [link withheld for anonymous review].
:::

::: {.content-visible unless-meta="blinded"} 
The study was approved by the Research Ethics Committee of the Faculty of Social Sciences, University of Chile (reference N° 10-09/2021), which determined that the project posed minimal risk and approved the consent and confidentiality procedures. 
The hypotheses were preregistered on the Open Science Framework (OSF) prior to data analysis (https://osf.io/2uhrk/overview)[https://osf.io/2uhrk/overview].
:::

## Measures

### Perceptions and preferences of meritocracy and privilege

Meritocratic and privilege-based perceptions and preferences were measured with the eight items of the original scale [@castillo_multidimensional_2023], two per dimension :

- Meritocratic perceptions: the extent to which effort and ability are rewarded in Chile,

- Privilege perceptions: the extent to which success is perceived as linked to connections and family wealth,

- Meritocratic preferences: agreement that those who work harder or are more talented should be better rewarded,

- Privilege acceptance: agreement that it is acceptable for individuals with better connections or wealthy parents to achieve greater success.
 
Each item (see @tbl-merit) is rated on a four-point scale ranging from “strongly disagree” (1) to “strongly agree” (4); higher scores indicate stronger endorsement of the corresponding perception or preference. The original Spanish wording of the items is reported in the supplementary material.

```{r}
#| tbl-cap: Items for perceptions and preferences for meritocracy scale
#| tbl-cap-location: top
#| tbl-pos: H
#| echo: false
#| label: tbl-merit


 tabitems <- read.csv(file = "../input/tables/table-01.csv",header = 1,sep = ",",encoding = "UTF-8") # call file  generated externally for the table
 tabitems <- tabitems[, 1:3] # original Spanish wording is reported in the supplementary material
 cnames <- c("Components", "Dimensions","Item")

 knitr::kable(tabitems, format = table_format, booktabs = T, linesep = "",col.names = cnames) %>%
   kableExtra::kable_styling(
     full_width = F,
     latex_options = c("HOLD_position", "scale_down"),
     position = "center",
     font_size = 9.5,
     bootstrap_options=c("striped", "bordered")) %>%
   kableExtra::column_spec(column = 1, width = "2cm", ) %>%
   kableExtra::column_spec(column = 2,width = "2 cm") %>%
   kableExtra::column_spec(column = 3, width = "10cm") %>%
   kableExtra::collapse_rows(columns = 1:2,valign = "middle")
```



## Analytical strategy

### Measurement model and estimation

We estimated a Confirmatory Factor Analysis (CFA) with four latent factors, using Diagonally Weighted Least Squares with robust correction (WLSMV), which is suitable for ordinal items [@kline_principles_2023]. Model fit was assessed following the guidelines of Brown [-@brown_confirmatory_2015], using several indices: the Comparative Fit Index (CFI) and the Tucker-Lewis Index (TLI), with acceptable values above 0.95; the Root Mean Square Error of Approximation (RMSEA), with values below 0.06 indicating good fit; and the Chi-square statistic (acceptable fit indicated by $p$ > 0.05 and a Chi-square/df ratio < 3).

### Measurement equivalence across time and groups

A key contribution of this study is its assessment of measurement stability through factorial invariance testing [@putnick_measurement_2016], both as a robust test of the measurement model and as a precondition for valid comparisons: only if the same latent construct is measured in the same way across time and groups can observed differences be attributed to true differences rather than measurement artifacts. We tested invariance across the two panel waves (longitudinal invariance) and across school levels (primary vs. secondary cohorts), comparing nested models with increasing constraints: configural (same factor structure), weak (equal factor loadings), strong (also equal thresholds/intercepts), and strict (also equal residual variances).

We followed recent recommendations for ordered categorical indicators, specifically the longitudinal approach of @liu_testing_2017 and the cross-group procedure as suggested by @svetina_multiplegroup_2020. In addition to the chi-square difference test, we assessed invariance using changes in approximate fit indices. Following @chen_sensitivity_2007, we treated $\Delta$CFI as the primary criterion and $\Delta$RMSEA as supplementary, indicating noninvariance when $\Delta$CFI $\leq$ −0.010 and $\Delta$RMSEA $\geq$ 0.015.

All analyses were performed using the *lavaan* package [@rosseel_Lavaan_2012] in R version 4.5.2.


