```

## Multidimensional measurement of meritocracy and privilege

```{r setup-measurement}
#| echo: false
#| message: false
#| warning: false
#| results: false

library(knitr)

knitr::opts_chunk$set(echo = F,
                      warning = F,
                      error = F, 
                      message = F) 

table_format <- if (is_html_output()) {
  "html"
} else if (is_latex_output()) {
  "latex"
}
table_format2 <- if (is_html_output()) {
  T
} else if (is_latex_output()) {
  F
}

options(kableExtra.html.bsTable = T)
options(knitr.kable.NA = "")

if (!require("pacman")) install.packages("pacman") #para instalar pacman en caso de no estar instalado

pacman::p_load(
  tidyverse,
  sjmisc,
  sjPlot,
  here,
  lavaan,
  psych,
  ggdist,
  semTools,
  gtools,
  kableExtra
)

# Silence only the known Heywood warning from lavaan while preserving all others
quiet_heywood <- function(expr) {
  withCallingHandlers(
    expr,
    warning = function(w) {
      if (grepl("some estimated ov variances are negative", conditionMessage(w), fixed = TRUE)) {
        invokeRestart("muffleWarning")
      }
    }
  )
}
run_cfa <- function(...) quiet_heywood(cfa(...))

options(scipen = 999) # para eliminar la notación científica

load(file = here("output", "data", "db_long_proc.RData")) #cargar base con las dos olas 

names(db_long) #ver nombres de variables
glimpse(db_long) # ver los primeros 15 casos de cada variable
dim(db_long) # ver cantidad de filas y columnas 
```

```{r generate-data}
#| echo: false
#| results: false

db1 <- db_long %>%
select(id_estudiante, starts_with(c("perc", "pref"))) %>% 
  drop_na()#%>% # eliminar variables que no son parte del analisis
#na.omit() # listwise deletion

```


```{r pooled-model}
#| include: false 

model_base <- ('
perc_merit =~ perc_effort + perc_talent
perc_nmerit =~ perc_rich_parents + perc_contact
pref_merit =~ pref_effort + pref_talent
pref_nmerit =~ pref_rich_parents + pref_contact
')

mgeneral_cfa <- run_cfa(model = model_base, 
                   data = db1, 
                   estimator = "WLSMV",
                   ordered = T,
                   std.lv = F,
                   parameterization = "theta")

summary(mgeneral_cfa, standardized = TRUE, fit.measures = TRUE)
```


@fig-general presents the measurement model for the pooled sample (both waves and cohorts), with the four latent dimensions (circles) each measured by two observed indicators (rectangles). Fit is reasonable but not fully satisfactory (CFI = 0.97; TLI = 0.94; RMSEA = 0.07): the CFI falls within commonly accepted ranges, but the TLI and RMSEA do not meet conventional thresholds. Part of this misfit relates to differences across cohorts, examined in the multigroup analyses below.


```{r}
#| label: fig-general
#| fig-cap: Measurement model diagram (pooled sample)
#| fig-cap-location: top
#| echo: false
#| fig-width: 10
#| fig-height: 5.3
#| out-width: '100%'
#| cache.extra: !expr unname(tools::md5sum(here::here('processing', 'plot_cfa_diagram.R')))

# Path diagram of the pooled 4-factor CFA drawn from a lavaan fit object
source(here::here("processing", "plot_cfa_diagram.R"))
plot_cfa_diagram(mgeneral_cfa)
```


All standardized factor loadings are statistically significant and moderate to strong: effort ($\beta$ = 0.88) and talent ($\beta$ = 0.62) for meritocratic perceptions; effort ($\beta$ = 0.59) and talent ($\beta$ = 0.55) for meritocratic preferences, the weakest pair; contacts ($\beta$ = 0.88) and rich family ($\beta$ = 0.72) for perceived privilege; and contacts ($\beta$ = 0.84) and rich family ($\beta$ = 0.72) for privilege acceptance. Overall, the indicators are reasonably good measures of their respective latent constructs.

The latent correlations address $H_2$. Allowing the four dimensions to correlate opens the black box of beliefs that both common sense and the literature often treat as opposite poles of a single continuum. The dimensions are interrelated but far from perfectly aligned, and all correlations but one are positive. The only negative correlation is between meritocratic and privilege perceptions ($r$ = −0.21): perceiving society as more meritocratic goes with a lower recognition of the role of privilege. As among adults [@castillo_multidimensional_2023], meritocratic perceptions and preferences are positively correlated ($r$ = 0.14). The strongest association is between privilege perceptions and meritocratic preferences ($r$ = 0.48): students who recognize the role of privilege in society are more likely to endorse meritocratic ideals. Less intuitively, privilege acceptance is positively correlated with both meritocratic perceptions ($r$ = 0.13) and meritocratic preferences ($r$ = 0.15). Although modest, these last correlations indicate that merit and privilege beliefs are not simply opposites.




## Stability of meritocracy and privilege beliefs 

### Longitudinal invariance

```{r longitudinal-data, include=FALSE}
#| results: asis
#| echo: false
#| warning: false
#| output: false

# Crear base en formato wide con dummy de cohorte (0 = primaria, 1 = secundaria)
db_invariance <- db_long %>%
  group_by(id_estudiante) %>%
  mutate(
    cohort_level = first(cohort_level),
    cohort_dummy = case_when(
      cohort_level == "Primary" ~ 0,
      cohort_level == "Secondary" ~ 1,
      TRUE ~ NA_real_
    )
  ) %>%
  ungroup() %>%
  select(id_estudiante, cohort_dummy, ola, starts_with(c("perc", "pref"))) %>%
  pivot_wider(
    id_cols = c(id_estudiante, cohort_dummy),
    names_from = ola,
    names_glue = "{.value}{ola}",
    values_from = c(
      perc_effort, perc_talent,
      perc_rich_parents, perc_contact,
      pref_effort, pref_talent,
      pref_rich_parents, pref_contact
    )
  ) %>%
  na.omit() %>%
  rename_with(~ str_replace_all(., "_", ""))

# Reescalar variables y convertir a ordinales
db_invariance <- db_invariance %>% 
  mutate(
    across(
      .cols = -c(idestudiante, cohortdummy),
      .fns = ~ case_when(. == 1 ~ 0,
                         . == 2 ~ 1,
                         . == 3 ~ 2,
                         . == 4 ~ 3)
    )
  ) %>%
  mutate(
    across(
      .cols = -c(idestudiante),
      .fns = ~ ordered(.)
    )
  )
```

```{r, include=FALSE}
#| label: conf-model
#| tbl-cap: "Longitudinal Invariance Test"
#| results: asis
#| echo: false
#| warning: false

# First, define the configural model, using the repeated measures factors and
# indicators
baseline_model_smt <- ('

###########################################
# Definición de factores (1 marcador por factor)
###########################################

percmerit1  =~ 1*perceffort1     + perctalent1
percmerit2  =~ 1*perceffort2     + perctalent2

percnmerit1 =~ 1*percrichparents1 + perccontact1
percnmerit2 =~ 1*percrichparents2 + perccontact2

prefmerit1  =~ 1*prefeffort1     + preftalent1
prefmerit2  =~ 1*prefeffort2     + preftalent2

prefnmerit1 =~ 1*prefrichparents1 + prefcontact1
prefnmerit2 =~ 1*prefrichparents2 + prefcontact2


###########################################
# Covarianzas entre errores (mismo ítem, distintas olas)
###########################################

perceffort1 ~~ perceffort2
perctalent1 ~~ perctalent2

percrichparents1 ~~ percrichparents2
perccontact1     ~~ perccontact2

prefeffort1 ~~ prefeffort2
preftalent1 ~~ preftalent2

prefrichparents1 ~~ prefrichparents2
prefcontact1     ~~ prefcontact2
')



# Model Estimation
fit_baseline <- run_cfa(baseline_model_smt, data = db_invariance,
                    ordered = c("perceffort1", "perctalent1", "perceffort2", "perctalent2",
                                "percrichparents1", "perccontact1", "percrichparents2", "perccontact2",
                                "prefeffort1", "preftalent1", "prefeffort2", "preftalent2",
                                "prefrichparents1", "prefcontact1", "prefrichparents2", "prefcontact2"),
                    parameterization = "theta",
                    estimator = "WLSMV")
```

```{r, include=FALSE}
#| label: Weak-Model
#| tbl-cap: "Longitudinal Invariance Weak Model"
#| results: asis
#| echo: false
#| warning: false

loadinginv_model_smt <- ('

# Igualación de cargas (invarianza débil)

percmerit1  =~ 1*perceffort1 + pe_loading*perctalent1
percmerit2  =~ 1*perceffort2 + pe_loading*perctalent2

percnmerit1 =~ 1*percrichparents1 + prc_loading*perccontact1
percnmerit2 =~ 1*percrichparents2 + prc_loading*perccontact2

prefmerit1  =~ 1*prefeffort1 + pfe_loading*preftalent1
prefmerit2  =~ 1*prefeffort2 + pfe_loading*preftalent2

prefnmerit1 =~ 1*prefrichparents1 + pfrc_loading*prefcontact1
prefnmerit2 =~ 1*prefrichparents2 + pfrc_loading*prefcontact2


# Varianzas y covarianzas latentes

percmerit1  ~~ percmerit1 + percmerit2
percmerit2  ~~ percmerit2
percnmerit1 ~~ percnmerit1 + percnmerit2
percnmerit2 ~~ percnmerit2
prefmerit1  ~~ prefmerit1 + prefmerit2
prefmerit2  ~~ prefmerit2
prefnmerit1 ~~ prefnmerit1 + prefnmerit2
prefnmerit2 ~~ prefnmerit2


# Umbrales (uno igualado por ítem)

# perceffort1 | pe1t1*t1 + pe1t2*t2
# perceffort2 | pe2t1*t1 + pe2t2*t2
# perctalent1 | pt1t1*t1 + pt1t2*t2
# perctalent2 | pt2t1*t1 + pt2t2*t2
# percrichparents1 | prp1t1*t1 + prp1t2*t2
# percrichparents2 | prp2t1*t1 + prp2t2*t2
# perccontact1 | pc1t1*t1 + pc1t2*t2
# perccontact2 | pc2t1*t1 + pc2t2*t2
# prefeffort1 | pre1t1*t1 + pre1t2*t2
# prefeffort2 | pre2t1*t1 + pre2t2*t2
# preftalent1 | prt1t1*t1 + prt1t2*t2
# preftalent2 | prt2t1*t1 + prt2t2*t2
# prefrichparents1 | pfrp1t1*t1 + pfrp1t2*t2
# prefrichparents2 | pfrp2t1*t1 + pfrp2t2*t2
# prefcontact1 | pfc1t1*t1 + pfc1t2*t2
# prefcontact2 | pfc2t1*t1 + pfc2t2*t2
 

# Covarianzas entre errores

perceffort1 ~~ perceffort2
perctalent1 ~~ perctalent2
percrichparents1 ~~ percrichparents2
perccontact1 ~~ perccontact2
prefeffort1 ~~ prefeffort2
preftalent1 ~~ preftalent2
prefrichparents1 ~~ prefrichparents2
prefcontact1 ~~ prefcontact2
')


fit_loadinginv <- run_cfa(loadinginv_model_smt, data = db_invariance,
                      ordered = c("perceffort1", "perctalent1", "perceffort2", "perctalent2",
                                  "percrichparents1", "perccontact1", "percrichparents2", "perccontact2",
                                  "prefeffort1", "preftalent1", "prefeffort2", "preftalent2",
                                  "prefrichparents1", "prefcontact1", "prefrichparents2", "prefcontact2"),
                      parameterization = "theta",
                      estimator = "WLSMV")
```

```{r, include=FALSE}
#| label: strong-model
#| tbl-cap: "Longitudinal Invariance Strong Model"
#| results: asis
#| echo: false
#| warning: false

thresholdinv_model_smt <- ('

# Igualación de cargas (como en el modelo débil)

percmerit1  =~ 1*perceffort1 + pe_loading*perctalent1
percmerit2  =~ 1*perceffort2 + pe_loading*perctalent2

percnmerit1 =~ 1*percrichparents1 + prc_loading*perccontact1
percnmerit2 =~ 1*percrichparents2 + prc_loading*perccontact2

prefmerit1  =~ 1*prefeffort1 + pfe_loading*preftalent1
prefmerit2  =~ 1*prefeffort2 + pfe_loading*preftalent2

prefnmerit1 =~ 1*prefrichparents1 + pfrc_loading*prefcontact1
prefnmerit2 =~ 1*prefrichparents2 + pfrc_loading*prefcontact2


# Varianzas y covarianzas latentes

percmerit1  ~~ percmerit1 + percmerit2
percmerit2  ~~ percmerit2
percnmerit1 ~~ percnmerit1 + percnmerit2
percnmerit2 ~~ percnmerit2
prefmerit1  ~~ prefmerit1 + prefmerit2
prefmerit2  ~~ prefmerit2
prefnmerit1 ~~ prefnmerit1 + prefnmerit2
prefnmerit2 ~~ prefnmerit2


# Thresholds iguales entre olas (invarianza fuerte)

perceffort1     | pe_t1*t1 + pe_t2*t2
perceffort2     | pe_t1*t1 + pe_t2*t2
perctalent1     | pt_t1*t1 + pt_t2*t2
perctalent2     | pt_t1*t1 + pt_t2*t2
percrichparents1| prp_t1*t1 + prp_t2*t2
percrichparents2| prp_t1*t1 + prp_t2*t2
perccontact1    | pc_t1*t1 + pc_t2*t2
perccontact2    | pc_t1*t1 + pc_t2*t2
prefeffort1     | pre_t1*t1 + pre_t2*t2
prefeffort2     | pre_t1*t1 + pre_t2*t2
preftalent1     | prt_t1*t1 + prt_t2*t2
preftalent2     | prt_t1*t1 + prt_t2*t2
prefrichparents1| pfrp_t1*t1 + pfrp_t2*t2
prefrichparents2| pfrp_t1*t1 + pfrp_t2*t2
prefcontact1    | pfc_t1*t1 + pfc_t2*t2
prefcontact2    | pfc_t1*t1 + pfc_t2*t2


# Interceptos fijos

perceffort1 + perctalent1 ~ 0*1
perceffort2 + perctalent2 ~ 0*1
percrichparents1 + perccontact1 ~ 0*1
percrichparents2 + perccontact2 ~ 0*1
prefeffort1 + preftalent1 ~ 0*1
prefeffort2 + preftalent2 ~ 0*1
prefrichparents1 + prefcontact1 ~ 0*1
prefrichparents2 + prefcontact2 ~ 0*1


# Varianzas únicas

perceffort1 ~~ 1*perceffort1
perctalent1 ~~ 1*perctalent1
percrichparents1 ~~ 1*percrichparents1
perccontact1 ~~ 1*perccontact1
prefeffort1 ~~ 1*prefeffort1
preftalent1 ~~ 1*preftalent1
prefrichparents1 ~~ 1*prefrichparents1
prefcontact1 ~~ 1*prefcontact1

perceffort2 ~~ NA*perceffort2
perctalent2 ~~ NA*perctalent2
percrichparents2 ~~ NA*percrichparents2
perccontact2 ~~ NA*perccontact2
prefeffort2 ~~ NA*prefeffort2
preftalent2 ~~ NA*preftalent2
prefrichparents2 ~~ NA*prefrichparents2
prefcontact2 ~~ NA*prefcontact2


# Covarianzas entre errores longitudinales

perceffort1 ~~ perceffort2
perctalent1 ~~ perctalent2
percrichparents1 ~~ percrichparents2
perccontact1 ~~ perccontact2
prefeffort1 ~~ prefeffort2
preftalent1 ~~ preftalent2
prefrichparents1 ~~ prefrichparents2
prefcontact1 ~~ prefcontact2
')

fit_thresholdinv <- run_cfa(thresholdinv_model_smt, data = db_invariance,
                        ordered = c("perceffort1", "perctalent1", "perceffort2", "perctalent2",
                                    "percrichparents1", "perccontact1", "percrichparents2", "perccontact2",
                                    "prefeffort1", "preftalent1", "prefeffort2", "preftalent2",
                                    "prefrichparents1", "prefcontact1", "prefrichparents2", "prefcontact2"),
                        parameterization = "theta",
                        estimator = "WLSMV")
```

```{r, include=FALSE}
#| label: strict-model
#| tbl-cap: "Longitudinal Invariance Strict Model"
#| results: asis
#| echo: false
#| warning: false

uniquenessinv_model_smt <- ('

# Cargas factoriales (invarianza débil)

percmerit1  =~ 1*perceffort1 + pe_loading*perctalent1
percmerit2  =~ 1*perceffort2 + pe_loading*perctalent2

percnmerit1 =~ 1*percrichparents1 + prc_loading*perccontact1
percnmerit2 =~ 1*percrichparents2 + prc_loading*perccontact2

prefmerit1  =~ 1*prefeffort1 + pfe_loading*preftalent1
prefmerit2  =~ 1*prefeffort2 + pfe_loading*preftalent2

prefnmerit1 =~ 1*prefrichparents1 + pfrc_loading*prefcontact1
prefnmerit2 =~ 1*prefrichparents2 + pfrc_loading*prefcontact2


# Varianzas y covarianzas de factores latentes

percmerit1  ~~ percmerit1 + percmerit2
percmerit2  ~~ percmerit2
percnmerit1 ~~ percnmerit1 + percnmerit2
percnmerit2 ~~ percnmerit2
prefmerit1  ~~ prefmerit1 + prefmerit2
prefmerit2  ~~ prefmerit2
prefnmerit1 ~~ prefnmerit1 + prefnmerit2
prefnmerit2 ~~ prefnmerit2


# Medias latentes

percmerit1  ~ 0*1
percmerit2  ~ 1
percnmerit1 ~ 0*1
percnmerit2 ~ 1
prefmerit1  ~ 0*1
prefmerit2  ~ 1
prefnmerit1 ~ 0*1
prefnmerit2 ~ 1


# Umbrales (invarianza fuerte)

perceffort1     | pe_t1*t1 + pe_t2*t2
perceffort2     | pe_t1*t1 + pe_t2*t2
perctalent1     | pt_t1*t1 + pt_t2*t2
perctalent2     | pt_t1*t1 + pt_t2*t2
percrichparents1| prp_t1*t1 + prp_t2*t2
percrichparents2| prp_t1*t1 + prp_t2*t2
perccontact1    | pc_t1*t1 + pc_t2*t2
perccontact2    | pc_t1*t1 + pc_t2*t2
prefeffort1     | pre_t1*t1 + pre_t2*t2
prefeffort2     | pre_t1*t1 + pre_t2*t2
preftalent1     | prt_t1*t1 + prt_t2*t2
preftalent2     | prt_t1*t1 + prt_t2*t2
prefrichparents1| pfrp_t1*t1 + pfrp_t2*t2
prefrichparents2| pfrp_t1*t1 + pfrp_t2*t2
prefcontact1    | pfc_t1*t1 + pfc_t2*t2
prefcontact2    | pfc_t1*t1 + pfc_t2*t2


# Interceptos fijos

perceffort1 + perctalent1 ~ 0*1
perceffort2 + perctalent2 ~ 0*1
percrichparents1 + perccontact1 ~ 0*1
percrichparents2 + perccontact2 ~ 0*1
prefeffort1 + preftalent1 ~ 0*1
prefeffort2 + preftalent2 ~ 0*1
prefrichparents1 + prefcontact1 ~ 0*1
prefrichparents2 + prefcontact2 ~ 0*1


# Varianzas únicas (invarianza estricta)
# Fijadas a 1 en todas las olas

perceffort1 ~~ 1*perceffort1
perceffort2 ~~ 1*perceffort2
perctalent1 ~~ 1*perctalent1
perctalent2 ~~ 1*perctalent2

percrichparents1 ~~ 1*percrichparents1
percrichparents2 ~~ 1*percrichparents2
perccontact1 ~~ 1*perccontact1
perccontact2 ~~ 1*perccontact2

prefeffort1 ~~ 1*prefeffort1
prefeffort2 ~~ 1*prefeffort2
preftalent1 ~~ 1*preftalent1
preftalent2 ~~ 1*preftalent2

prefrichparents1 ~~ 1*prefrichparents1
prefrichparents2 ~~ 1*prefrichparents2
prefcontact1 ~~ 1*prefcontact1
prefcontact2 ~~ 1*prefcontact2


# Covarianzas entre errores (mismo ítem, diferente ola)

perceffort1 ~~ perceffort2
perctalent1 ~~ perctalent2
percrichparents1 ~~ percrichparents2
perccontact1 ~~ perccontact2
prefeffort1 ~~ prefeffort2
preftalent1 ~~ preftalent2
prefrichparents1 ~~ prefrichparents2
prefcontact1 ~~ prefcontact2
')

fit_uniquenessinv <- run_cfa(uniquenessinv_model_smt, data = db_invariance,
                         ordered = c("perceffort1", "perctalent1", "perceffort2", "perctalent2",
                                     "percrichparents1", "perccontact1", "percrichparents2", "perccontact2",
                                     "prefeffort1", "preftalent1", "prefeffort2", "preftalent2",
                                     "prefrichparents1", "prefcontact1", "prefrichparents2", "prefcontact2"),
                         parameterization = "theta",
                         estimator = "WLSMV")
```

@tbl-longinv reports the longitudinal invariance tests, in which the same students measured at two time points are treated as groups and the unique variances of each item are correlated across waves [@widaman_factorial_2010]. The scale achieved the strongest level, strict invariance: the strict model fit well, $\chi^2$(84) = 130.17, CFI = 0.991, RMSEA = 0.031 (90% CI: 0.020–0.040), and its fit was practically identical to that of the strong model, $\Delta \chi^2$(4) = 1.635, $\Delta$CFI = 0.000, $\Delta$RMSEA = −0.002, well within commonly used cutoffs [@chen_sensitivity_2007].

```{r}
#| tbl-cap: "Longitudinal invariance results"
#| tbl-cap-location: top
#| tbl-pos: H
#| echo: false
#| warning: false
#| label: tbl-longinv

# Compare fit

an1 <- anova(fit_baseline, fit_loadinginv)
an2 <- anova(fit_loadinginv, fit_thresholdinv)
an3 <- anova(fit_thresholdinv, fit_uniquenessinv)

tab01 <- bind_rows(
  as_tibble(an1)[2,],
  as_tibble(an2)[2,],
  as_tibble(an3)[2,]
) %>%
  select("Chisq", "Df", chisq_diff = `Chisq diff`, df_diff = `Df diff`, pvalue = `Pr(>Chisq)`) %>%
  mutate(
    stars = stars.pval(pvalue),
    chisqt = paste0(round(Chisq, 2), " (", Df, ")"),
    decision = ifelse(pvalue > 0.05, "Accept", "Reject"),
    model = c("Weak", "Strong", "Strict")
  ) %>%
  bind_rows(
    tibble(Chisq = an1$Chisq[1], Df = an1$Df[1], chisq_diff = NA, df_diff = NA,
           pvalue = NA, stars = "", chisqt = paste0(round(an1$Chisq[1], 2), " (", an1$Df[1], ")"),
           decision = "Reference", model = "Configural")
  ) %>%
  select(model, chisqt, chisq_diff, df_diff, pvalue, stars, decision) %>%
  mutate(model = factor(model, levels = c("Configural", "Weak", "Strong", "Strict"))) %>%
  arrange(model)


fit.meas <- dplyr::bind_rows(lavaan::fitmeasures(fit_baseline, output ="matrix")[c("chisq","df","cfi","rmsea","rmsea.ci.lower","rmsea.ci.upper"),],
                             lavaan::fitmeasures(fit_loadinginv,  output ="matrix")[c("chisq","df","cfi","rmsea","rmsea.ci.lower","rmsea.ci.upper"),],
                             lavaan::fitmeasures(fit_thresholdinv, output ="matrix")[c("chisq","df","cfi","rmsea","rmsea.ci.lower","rmsea.ci.upper"),],
                             lavaan::fitmeasures(fit_uniquenessinv, output ="matrix")[c("chisq","df","cfi","rmsea","rmsea.ci.lower","rmsea.ci.upper"),])

fit.meas <- fit.meas %>%
  dplyr::mutate(diff.chi2 = chisq    - lag(chisq,default = dplyr::first(chisq)),
                diff.df   = df       - lag(df,   default = dplyr::first(df)),
                diff.cfi  = cfi      - lag(cfi,  default = dplyr::first(cfi)),
                diff.rmsea   = rmsea - lag(rmsea,default = dplyr::first(rmsea))) %>%
  round(3) %>%
  dplyr::mutate(rmsea.ci=paste0(rmsea," \n ", "(",rmsea.ci.lower,"-",rmsea.ci.upper,")"))

tab.inv <- dplyr::bind_cols(tab01,fit.meas) %>%
  dplyr::select(model,chisqt,cfi,rmsea.ci,diff.chi2,diff.df,diff.cfi,diff.rmsea,stars,decision) %>%
  dplyr::mutate(diff.chi2=paste0(diff.chi2," (",diff.df,") ",stars)) %>%
  dplyr::select(model,chisqt,cfi,rmsea.ci,diff.chi2,diff.cfi,diff.rmsea,decision)


col.nam <- if (is_html_output()) {
  c("Model", "&chi;<sup>2</sup> (df)", "CFI", "RMSEA (90% CI)",
    "&Delta;&chi;<sup>2</sup> (&Delta;df)", "&Delta;CFI", "&Delta;RMSEA", "Decision")
} else {
  c("Model", "$\\chi^2$ (df)", "CFI", "RMSEA (90\\% CI)",
    "$\\Delta \\chi^2$ ($\\Delta$df)", "$\\Delta$CFI", "$\\Delta$RMSEA", "Decision")
}

tab.inv %>%
  kableExtra::kable(format = table_format,
                    align = "c",
                    booktabs = T,
                    escape = F,
                    caption = NULL,
                    col.names = col.nam) %>%
  kableExtra::kable_styling(full_width = F,
                            latex_options = c("HOLD_position", "scale_down"),
                            bootstrap_options=c("striped", "bordered", "condensed"),
                           font_size = 9.5) %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 1, width = "2cm") %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 2, width = "2.1cm") %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 3, width = "1.3cm") %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 4, width = "2.2cm") %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 5, width = "2.4cm") %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 6, width = "1.3cm") %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 7, width = "1.4cm") %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 8, width = "2cm")
```

```{r}
#| label: latent-means-waves
#| include: false

# Latent means by wave, taken from the strict-invariance model: the means of
# wave 1 are fixed to 0 (reference) and those of wave 2 are freely estimated,
# so the wave 2 estimate is the wave 2 - wave 1 difference, tested with a Wald z.
factors_lm <- c(percmerit = "Perceived meritocracy (effort and talent)",
                percnmerit = "Perceived privilege (rich parents and contacts)",
                prefmerit = "Preference for meritocracy (effort and talent)",
                prefnmerit = "Acceptance of privilege (rich parents and contacts)")

pe_lm <- parameterEstimates(fit_uniquenessinv)

lm_t2 <- pe_lm %>%
  filter(op == "~1", lhs %in% paste0(names(factors_lm), "2")) %>%
  transmute(factor = sub("2$", "", lhs), diff = est, se = se, z = z, p = pvalue,
            ci.lower, ci.upper)

# Latent SD in wave 1 to express the difference in standardized units
lm_sd1 <- pe_lm %>%
  filter(op == "~~", lhs == rhs, lhs %in% paste0(names(factors_lm), "1")) %>%
  transmute(factor = sub("1$", "", lhs), sd1 = sqrt(est))

tab_lm <- lm_t2 %>%
  left_join(lm_sd1, by = "factor") %>%
  mutate(d = diff / sd1,
         p_holm = p.adjust(p, method = "holm"),
         label = factors_lm[factor],
         mean_w1 = 0) %>%
  arrange(match(factor, names(factors_lm)))

# Omnibus check: constraining the four wave-2 means to 0 (= wave-1 means)
model_equalmeans <- gsub("((?:perc|pref)n?merit2)\\s+~ 1\\n", "\\1 ~ 0*1\n",
                         uniquenessinv_model_smt, perl = TRUE)

fit_equalmeans <- run_cfa(model_equalmeans, data = db_invariance,
                          ordered = c("perceffort1", "perctalent1", "perceffort2", "perctalent2",
                                      "percrichparents1", "perccontact1", "percrichparents2", "perccontact2",
                                      "prefeffort1", "preftalent1", "prefeffort2", "preftalent2",
                                      "prefrichparents1", "prefcontact1", "prefrichparents2", "prefcontact2"),
                          parameterization = "theta",
                          estimator = "WLSMV")

lrt_equalmeans <- lavTestLRT(fit_uniquenessinv, fit_equalmeans)
```

Longitudinal invariance implies that changes in students' scores can be interpreted as true changes in their beliefs rather than artifacts of measurement. @tbl-latmeans reports the latent means from the strict model, with wave 1 means fixed to zero, so that the wave 2 estimates are the between-wave changes, tested with Wald tests. Latent means are in the metric of each factor's first indicator, and $d$ expresses the change in wave 1 latent standard deviations. No dimension changed significantly between waves (all adjusted $p$ > .30). Perceived meritocracy declined slightly ($\Delta$ = −0.29, $p$ = .118, $d$ = −0.14) and perceived privilege increased slightly ($\Delta$ = 0.15, $p$ = .080, $d$ = 0.13), a direction compatible with a growing perception of privilege, but neither difference is reliably different from zero. Preferences were practically unchanged, both for meritocracy ($\Delta$ = −0.08, $p$ = .458) and for the acceptance of privilege ($\Delta$ = −0.12, $p$ = .119). Overall, students' beliefs were stable over the period.

```{r}
#| tbl-cap: "Latent means of meritocracy and privilege dimensions by wave"
#| tbl-cap-location: top
#| tbl-pos: H
#| echo: false
#| warning: false
#| label: tbl-latmeans

tab_lm_out <- tab_lm %>%
  transmute(
    label,
    mean_w1 = "0 (ref.)",
    mean_w2 = sprintf("%.3f (%.3f)", diff, se),
    z = sprintf("%.2f", z),
    p = ifelse(p < .001, "< .001", sub("^0", "", sprintf("%.3f", p))),
    p_holm = ifelse(p_holm < .001, "< .001", sub("^0", "", sprintf("%.3f", p_holm))),
    d = sprintf("%.2f", d)
  )

col.nam.lm <- if (is_html_output()) {
  c("Dimension", "Wave 1", "Wave 2 (SE)", "<i>z</i>", "<i>p</i>", "<i>p</i> (Holm)", "<i>d</i>")
} else {
  c("Dimension", "Wave 1", "Wave 2 (SE)", "$z$", "$p$", "$p$ (Holm)", "$d$")
}

tab_lm_out %>%
  kableExtra::kable(format = table_format,
                    align = c("l", rep("c", 6)),
                    booktabs = T,
                    escape = F,
                    caption = NULL,
                    col.names = col.nam.lm) %>%
  kableExtra::kable_styling(full_width = F,
                            latex_options = c("HOLD_position", "scale_down"),
                            bootstrap_options = c("striped", "bordered", "condensed"),
                            font_size = 9.5) %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 1, width = "5.5cm")
```

::: {style="font-size: 0.8em; margin-top: -1em;"}
`\vspace{-1.2em}\footnotesize`{=latex} *Note:* Latent means from the strict longitudinal invariance model ($N$ = 591); wave 1 means are fixed to 0 (reference). Wave 2 estimates are the wave 2 − wave 1 differences, tested with Wald tests; $p$ (Holm) adjusts for the four comparisons. $d$ = difference divided by the latent standard deviation in wave 1. `\par\normalsize`{=latex}
:::

## Cohorts comparability


```{r}
#| label: trad-cohort-invariance
#| include: false
#| warning: false
#| message: false

# Traditional multigroup invariance with native lavaan (wave 1, primary vs secondary)
# For ordinal indicators: configural -> loadings -> loadings + thresholds

db_trad <- db_long %>%
  filter(ola == 1) %>%
  select(id_estudiante, starts_with(c("perc", "pref")), cohort_level) %>%
  drop_na()

model_cohort <- '
perc_merit =~ perc_effort + perc_talent
perc_nmerit =~ perc_rich_parents + perc_contact
pref_merit =~ pref_effort + pref_talent
pref_nmerit =~ pref_rich_parents + pref_contact
'

ord_items <- c("perc_effort", "perc_talent", "perc_rich_parents", "perc_contact",
               "pref_effort", "pref_talent", "pref_rich_parents", "pref_contact")

trad_configural <- cfa(model_cohort, data = db_trad, group = "cohort_level",
                       ordered = ord_items, estimator = "WLSMV")

trad_loadings <- cfa(model_cohort, data = db_trad, group = "cohort_level",
                     ordered = ord_items, estimator = "WLSMV",
                     group.equal = "loadings")

trad_thresholds <- cfa(model_cohort, data = db_trad, group = "cohort_level",
                       ordered = ord_items, estimator = "WLSMV",
                       group.equal = c("loadings", "thresholds"))

# chi-square difference tests and fit indices
lavTestLRT(trad_configural, trad_loadings, trad_thresholds)

sapply(list(configural = trad_configural, loadings = trad_loadings, thresholds = trad_thresholds),
       fitMeasures, fit.measures = c("chisq.scaled", "df.scaled", "cfi.scaled", "rmsea.scaled"))

#                 Df AIC BIC  Chisq Chisq diff    RMSEA Df diff Pr(>Chisq)    
# trad_configural 28         30.368                                           
# trad_loadings   32         48.890     20.769 0.092642       4  0.0003518 ***
# trad_thresholds 40         68.130     35.890 0.057633       8  1.839e-05 ***

# No invariance

# --- Sources of non-invariance ---------------------------------------------
# 1) Score tests on the loadings-constrained model: one test per equality constraint
score_loadings <- lavTestScore(trad_loadings)
score_loadings$test                 # joint test of all constraints

pt <- parTable(trad_loadings)       # map constraint labels (.pN.) to parameters
score_loadings$uni %>%
  left_join(pt %>% filter(group == 1) %>% select(plabel, lhs.par = lhs, rhs.par = rhs),
            by = c("lhs" = "plabel")) %>%
  mutate(p.holm = p.adjust(p.value, "holm")) %>%
  arrange(p.value)

# 2) Group-specific loadings in the configural model (where do they differ?)
parameterEstimates(trad_configural, standardized = TRUE) %>%
  filter(op == "=~") %>%
  select(lhs, rhs, group, est, std.all)

# 3) Partial invariance: free the offending loading (pref_talent), keep the rest equal
trad_partial_load <- cfa(model_cohort, data = db_trad, group = "cohort_level",
                         ordered = ord_items, estimator = "WLSMV",
                         group.equal = "loadings",
                         group.partial = "pref_merit =~ pref_talent")

trad_partial_thres <- cfa(model_cohort, data = db_trad, group = "cohort_level",
                          ordered = ord_items, estimator = "WLSMV",
                          group.equal = c("loadings", "thresholds"),
                          group.partial = "pref_merit =~ pref_talent")

lavTestLRT(trad_configural, trad_partial_load, trad_partial_thres)

sapply(list(configural = trad_configural, partial_loadings = trad_partial_load,
            partial_thresholds = trad_partial_thres),
       fitMeasures, fit.measures = c("chisq.scaled", "df.scaled", "cfi.scaled", "rmsea.scaled"))

lavTestScore(trad_partial_thres)$test   # joint test of the remaining constraints

# 4) Which thresholds drive the misfit? Univariate score tests by item
score_thres <- lavTestScore(trad_partial_thres)
lab_thres <- parTable(trad_partial_thres) %>%
  filter(group == 1) %>%
  transmute(plabel, item = lhs, threshold = rhs)

score_thres$uni %>%
  left_join(lab_thres, by = c("lhs" = "plabel")) %>%
  mutate(p.holm = p.adjust(p.value, "holm")) %>%
  arrange(p.value) %>%
  select(item, threshold, X2, p.value, p.holm)
```

@tbl-cohortinv reports the cross-cohort (multigroup) invariance tests comparing primary and secondary students, using wave 1 data. The configural model, which imposes the same four-factor structure in both cohorts, fits well, $\chi^2$(28) = 47.65, CFI = 0.991, RMSEA = 0.041. Weak invariance, however, was not achieved: constraining the factor loadings to be equal significantly worsened fit, $\Delta \chi^2$(4) = 20.77, $p$ < .001, although the changes in CFI ($\Delta$CFI = −0.009) and RMSEA ($\Delta$RMSEA = 0.013) remain just within conventional cutoffs [@chen_sensitivity_2007], so at the global level the evidence against loading invariance is not overwhelming. Adding equality constraints on the thresholds, which play the role of intercepts for ordinal items, worsened fit further, $\Delta \chi^2$(8) = 35.89, $p$ < .001, with $\Delta$CFI = −0.011, which exceeds the recommended cutoff. The alternative sequence proposed by Svetina et al. [-@svetina_multiplegroup_2020], which tests thresholds before loadings, led to the same conclusion (see supplementary material). The relationship between the latent factors and their indicators therefore differs between primary and secondary students, which could reflect developmental differences or differences in how the items are interpreted.

```{r}
#| tbl-cap: "Cohort (multigroup) invariance results"
#| tbl-cap-location: top
#| tbl-pos: H
#| echo: false
#| warning: false
#| message: false
#| label: tbl-cohortinv

# Models and the model each one is compared against (NA = reference)
fit_coh <- list(
  "Configural"                     = trad_configural,
  "Loadings"                       = trad_loadings,
  "Loadings + Thresholds"          = trad_thresholds,
  "Loadings (partial)"             = trad_partial_load,
  "Loadings (partial) + Thresholds" = trad_partial_thres
)
ref_coh <- c(NA, 1, 2, 1, 4)

fi_coh <- c("chisq.scaled", "df.scaled", "cfi.scaled", "rmsea.scaled",
            "rmsea.ci.lower.scaled", "rmsea.ci.upper.scaled")

fm_coh <- t(sapply(fit_coh, fitMeasures, fit.measures = fi_coh)) %>%
  as.data.frame() %>%
  rename_with(~ str_remove(., ".scaled"))

# scaled chi-square difference tests against the reference model of each row
lrt_coh <- purrr::map_dfr(seq_along(fit_coh), function(i) {
  if (is.na(ref_coh[i])) return(tibble(dchi = NA_real_, ddf = NA_real_, pvalue = NA_real_))
  a <- suppressMessages(suppressWarnings(
    lavTestLRT(fit_coh[[ref_coh[i]]], fit_coh[[i]])[2, ]
  ))
  tibble(dchi = a$`Chisq diff`, ddf = a$`Df diff`, pvalue = a$`Pr(>Chisq)`)
})

tab_coh <- bind_cols(model = names(fit_coh), fm_coh, lrt_coh) %>%
  mutate(
    diff.cfi   = cfi - cfi[ref_coh],
    diff.rmsea = rmsea - rmsea[ref_coh],
    chisqt     = paste0(round(chisq, 2), " (", df, ")"),
    rmsea.ci   = paste0(round(rmsea, 3), " (", round(rmsea.ci.lower, 3), "-", round(rmsea.ci.upper, 3), ")"),
    diff.chi2  = ifelse(is.na(dchi), "",
                        paste0(round(dchi, 2), " (", ddf, ") ", stars.pval(pvalue))),
    decision   = case_when(is.na(pvalue) ~ "Reference",
                           pvalue > 0.05 ~ "Accept",
                           TRUE ~ "Reject"),
    cfi        = round(cfi, 3),
    diff.cfi   = ifelse(is.na(dchi), "", format(round(diff.cfi, 3), nsmall = 3)),
    diff.rmsea = ifelse(is.na(dchi), "", format(round(diff.rmsea, 3), nsmall = 3))
  ) %>%
  select(model, chisqt, cfi, rmsea.ci, diff.chi2, diff.cfi, diff.rmsea, decision)

col.nam <- if (is_html_output()) {
  c("Model", "&chi;<sup>2</sup> (df)", "CFI", "RMSEA (90% CI)",
    "&Delta;&chi;<sup>2</sup> (&Delta;df)", "&Delta;CFI", "&Delta;RMSEA", "Decision")
} else {
  c("Model", "$\\chi^2$ (df)", "CFI", "RMSEA (90\\% CI)",
    "$\\Delta \\chi^2$ ($\\Delta$df)", "$\\Delta$CFI", "$\\Delta$RMSEA", "Decision")
}

tab_coh_kbl <- tab_coh %>%
  kableExtra::kable(format = table_format,
                    align = "c",
                    booktabs = T,
                    escape = F,
                    caption = NULL,
                    col.names = col.nam) %>%
  kableExtra::kable_styling(full_width = F,
                            latex_options = c("HOLD_position", "scale_down"),
                            bootstrap_options = c("striped", "bordered", "condensed"),
                            font_size = 9.5) %>%
  kableExtra::pack_rows("Full invariance sequence", 1, 3) %>%
  kableExtra::pack_rows("Partial invariance (loading of preferences for talent freed)", 4, 5) %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 1, width = "3.6cm") %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 2, width = "2.1cm") %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 3, width = "1.3cm") %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 4, width = "2.2cm") %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 5, width = "2.4cm") %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 6, width = "1.3cm") %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 7, width = "1.4cm") %>%
  kableExtra::column_spec(column = 8, width = "2cm")

tab_note <- "Scaled WLSMV statistics. Each model is compared with the preceding one in its block (partial loadings: vs. configural)."

# note below the table, outside the cells, in a smaller font than the table (9.5pt)
if (is_html_output()) {
  knitr::asis_output(paste0(
    tab_coh_kbl,
    "<div style='font-size: 0.75em; text-align: left; margin-top: -0.9em;'><em>Note.</em> ",
    tab_note, "</div>"
  ))
} else {
  knitr::asis_output(paste0(
    tab_coh_kbl,
    "\n\\vspace{-1.2em}\n\\begingroup\\fontsize{8}{9.5}\\selectfont\\raggedright ",
    "\\textit{Note.} ", tab_note, "\\par\\endgroup\n"
  ))
}
```



To identify the source of non-invariance, we inspected univariate score tests for each equality constraint on the loadings. The misfit was concentrated in a single parameter: the loading of the talent preference item on the meritocratic preferences factor ($\chi^2$(1) = 16.87, Holm-adjusted $p$ < .001). The remaining loadings were fully consistent with invariance (all $p$ > .36). Accordingly, freeing that single loading is enough to restore weak invariance, with a fit indistinguishable from the configural model, $\Delta \chi^2$(3) = 1.82, $p$ = .61, CFI = 0.992, RMSEA = 0.037.

The cohort-specific loadings (see supplementary material) show what this means substantively. In primary school the meritocratic preferences factor is defined mainly by the preference for talent as a criterion of reward (standardized loading = 0.87) and much less by the preference for effort (0.45). In secondary school the pattern reverses: effort becomes the core of the factor (0.83) whereas talent is only weakly related to it (0.37). The meritocratic preferences factor thus does not carry an equivalent meaning across cohorts, which may reflect a change in how students interpret meritocratic principles over the school years, and its levels should be compared across cohorts with caution. In contrast, the loadings of the other three factors are equivalent across cohorts.

Even after freeing this loading, adding the threshold constraints significantly worsened fit ($\Delta \chi^2$(8) = 42.72, $p$ < .001; $\Delta$CFI = −0.014), so strong invariance was also not achieved. In this case, the source is different: univariate score tests point to the thresholds of the perceived effort item, for which all three constraints are violated ($\chi^2$(1) between 11.6 and 11.9; Holm-adjusted $p$ between .015 and .017), whereas no threshold of any other item shows meaningful misfit (adjusted $p$ > .99). Substantively, students in the two cohorts use the response categories of this item differently for a given level of perceived meritocracy, which is consistent with the differences reported in the descriptive section (see @fig-likerplot2): secondary students are more likely to endorse the lowest response category when judging whether effort is rewarded in society, suggesting that they apply different criteria. Taken together, only partial weak invariance holds, and comparisons of latent means across cohorts, in particular for meritocratic perceptions, should be interpreted with caution.



---
bibliography: "../input/bib/merit-factorial.bib"
csl: "../input/bib/apa6.csl"
---

# Discussion

Meritocracy and privilege are often treated as opposite poles of a single continuum, but the present study shows that they are better understood as distinct dimensions that coexist in students' minds, as found among adults [@castillo_multidimensional_2023]. As expected in $H_1$, the four latent constructs (perceived meritocracy, perceived privilege, meritocratic preferences, and privilege acceptance) are identifiable in the school-aged population, with satisfactory model fit and strong factor loadings in the two waves analyzed. Even at an early stage of school socialization, students distinguish between meritocratic and privilege-based perceptions and preferences. This structure resonates with qualitative evidence from Chilean schools, where students across socioeconomic groups simultaneously invoke meritocratic ideals and recognize structural advantage [@neutaguayo_neoliberalism_2025], and where merit is often defined by attitude and visible effort rather than by academic outcomes [@moyanodavila_merit_2025]. Where merit is primarily understood as a behavioral disposition, accepting that privilege leads to better outcomes may not be experienced as a contradiction but as a separate normative judgment, which suggests that the coexistence of these orientations reflects how students navigate competing narratives about fairness and reward, rather than an artifact of survey design.

The empirical separability of these dimensions, and the associations among them ($H_2$), have implications for how meritocratic beliefs are understood and measured [@kwon_multidimensionality_2024; @mijs_visualizing_2026; @zhu_meritocratic_2025]. First, perceived privilege is positively associated with stronger meritocratic preferences, consistent with a normative-reactive response to perceived unfairness [@castillo_multidimensional_2023]. Second, meritocratic perceptions are weakly correlated with privilege acceptance in the pooled sample, a pattern that is not consistent across cohorts and waves. Prior research has linked meritocratic perceptions to the self-interest hypothesis, whereby those in higher social positions are more likely to adopt a meritocratic worldview [@heuer_legitimizing_2020]. Incorporating privilege reveals a more complex picture: perceiving society as meritocratic may also conceal support for privilege, so that the justification of inequality is embedded in the meritocratic narrative itself [@khan_saying_2013]. These results are consistent with Zhu's [-@zhu_meritocratic_2025] argument that meritocratic and structural explanations accumulate rather than displace one another, with accounts in which merit endorsement remains stable as recognition of structural constraints increases [@liu_does_2025; @tang_meritocratic_2025], and with the distinct attitudinal clusters in which @kwon_multidimensionality_2024 show that coexistence takes different forms across individuals.

These results also bear on how the literature on school meritocracy should be read. Research has linked stronger meritocratic beliefs among students to inequality legitimation, weaker support for redistribution, and greater acceptance of unequal access to educational resources [@batruch_belief_2022; @castillo_socialization_2024; @wiederkehr_belief_2015], but mostly with unidimensional or undifferentiated measures that cannot establish whether these effects are specific to particular dimensions. Our findings suggest that they are likely dimension-specific: perceived meritocracy is not equivalent to preferred meritocracy, and neither carries the same consequences as perceived privilege or privilege acceptance. @chauvin_school_2026 similarly distinguish ability and equity, but their measure is confined to perceived meritocracy among teachers. The present study provides a multidimensional measurement of meritocratic and privilege beliefs applicable to students, which makes it possible to examine whether distinct dimensions carry distinct consequences for the educational environment, from students' sense of justice in school to civic attitudes and the broader climate of fairness.

Regarding $H_3$, strict longitudinal invariance indicates that the scale measures the same constructs across waves, so that changes over time reflect real within-student change rather than shifts in how items are interpreted [@liu_testing_2017; @putnick_measurement_2016]. Meritocratic belief structures appear fairly stable over one year in early-to-mid adolescence, which gives further studies in this agenda a sound measurement basis. Invariance across educational levels, however, was not supported. The non-invariance is concentrated in two parameters: the relative weight of talent and effort in meritocratic preferences, and how students read whether effort is rewarded. We do not consider this finding reducible to a mere measurement failure. Rather, we interpret it as older students attaching different meanings to these items, linked to their more sophisticated reasoning about fairness and to their growing exposure to school sorting such as grades, tracking, and peer comparison. The declining centrality of talent in older students' meritocratic preferences may reflect a gradual disenchantment with inherited attributes [@resh_sense_2014; @tang_meritocratic_2025]. This could be related to Chile's stratified system, where the gap between meritocratic discourse and structural reality can be experienced as a moral grievance [@neutaguayo_neoliberalism_2025], but research in other contexts could help shed light on this issue.




---
bibliography: "../input/bib/merit-factorial.bib"
csl: "../input/bib/apa6.csl"
---

# Conclusions

Schools socialize students into beliefs about meritocracy, promoting effort and talent as the legitimate basis of reward while simultaneously exposing them to the weight of family resources and social connections in shaping outcomes [@resh_sense_2014; @traini_stratification_2022; @goudeau_hidden_2017a]. Many students navigate this tension by holding both orientations at once, endorsing merit as an ideal while recognizing that privilege structures actual outcomes, a configuration that can be associated with _dual consciousness_ [@liu_does_2025; @tang_meritocratic_2025; @kluegel_beliefs_1987]. Extending Castillo et al.'s [-@castillo_multidimensional_2023] multidimensional framework to two cohorts of Chilean students (6th and 9th graders in 2023, followed into 7th and 10th grade in 2024), the measurement of meritocratic and privilege beliefs provides evidence of reliability in school-aged populations, and equivalently over time. This is a foundational contribution: a precondition for studying the developmental trajectory of meritocratic beliefs and their educational implications, such as gender and social class differences.

This study draws on a non-probability sample of nine schools in one country, so its results cannot be statistically generalized, but three of its contributions may be relevant beyond this setting. First, the distinctions between perceived and preferred meritocracy, and between meritocratic and privilege-based principles, may reflect cognitive and normative capacities that develop during adolescence, although whether the four-factor structure replicates in other societies remains to be established. Second, the coexistence of merit endorsement and recognition of privilege has been documented in China [@liu_does_2025; @tang_meritocratic_2025], the United States and Finland [@zhu_meritocratic_2025], and Europe [@kwon_multidimensionality_2024], suggesting it is not a Chilean peculiarity. Third, longitudinal invariance testing as a prerequisite for developmental inference applies to any panel study of meritocratic beliefs. By contrast, the cross-cohort non-invariance may be more system-dependent, reflecting the intensity and timing of school-based sorting in Chile's tracked and marketized system [@madariaga_three_2020; @valenzuela_socioeconomic_2013]; research in countries with different tracking regimes will help clarify which aspects of measurement equivalence are universal.

This study has limitations that also point to directions for future research. First, the scale relies on two items per factor, which restricts both content coverage and reliability. Second, the design comprises only two waves and two school levels; additional waves and a broader age range are needed to trace developmental trajectories and to establish when the meaning students attach to meritocracy begins to shift. Third, and most consequentially, the lack of invariance across school cohorts indicates that meritocracy scales cannot be assumed to function equivalently across educational stages. Future studies should therefore examine differential item functioning, develop stage-sensitive item formulations, and adopt partial-invariance approaches [@putnick_measurement_2016; @vandeschoot_checklist_2012].  This would allow a more rigorous examination of group differences, such as the gender differences in meritocratic beliefs suggested by @gogescu_meritocracy_2025 and @khan_saying_2013, and of how beliefs about merit and privilege relate to the school outcomes for which they are likely to matter most: students' sense of justice and fairness, civic attitudes and engagement, perceptions of school climate, wellbeing, and academic motivation.


